winebus: a deadband that measures what the game asked, not what the pad gets.

The band is four of 255 and it was compared against the value the GAME asked
for. The gain that decides what the pad actually receives is applied afterwards,
by mgvf-0009's rewrite. So the band meant four of 255 only at a strength of x1,
and at x10 it meant forty.

MEASURED ON THE TRACES OF 2026-09-11, the same sessions this option has been
tuned on all week:

                                        at x4          at x10
  changes held under the band            358            632
  movement held, median at the pad         4 of 255      10 of 255
  the largest held, at the pad            12 of 255      30 of 255
  held changes worth >= 10 at the pad     16%           52%
  held changes worth >= 25 at the pad      0            106

Raising the strength was making the rumble COARSER instead of stronger. Half of
everything the band threw away at x10 was a step the hand would have felt, and
a hundred and six of them were steps of a tenth of the motor's range. The
control says "strength" and what it was doing past the middle of its travel was
sanding the texture off. That is what a person holding the pad reported, twice,
before anybody looked at this line.

So the band compares the movement AT THE PAD now, through the same arithmetic
the rewrite will do -- which is why dualsense_scale_motor moves into the header.
Two copies of three lines that have to agree is how they stop agreeing.

AND THE SECOND HALF, WHICH IS THE ONE THAT NEEDS CARE, because the band is the
reason this driver stopped costing frames and nothing should be taken off it on
a hunch. mgvf-0018 took Beast of Reincarnation from 3-5 fps lost with visible
stutter to 2 fps and none, by not spending a slot on a link that drains about
sixty-five reports a second. Every change it held was a packet of OURS that did
not go out.

A RIDDEN PACKET IS NOT ONE OF OURS. dualsense_bt_set_output_report forwards the
client's 0x31 either way: stamped, from our copy; untouched, from the client's
own buffer. Same packet, same link, same slot, whichever branch runs. The band
on the ride cannot change how much is offered to the radio. All it can decide is
whether the motors inside a packet that is leaving anyway are current or stale,
and it was choosing stale sixty per cent of the time.

What taking it off does add is a CRC over 74 bytes on the packets it no longer
skips, on the thread carrying the client's write. At the sixty writes a second
measured that is arithmetic and not I/O, against a write whose median cost was
362 microseconds -- and what made that thread expensive was back-pressure on the
radio, which this adds none of.

So the band stays exactly where it earned its place, on the thread's own writes,
which really are slots. The ride carries everything, at the game's own rate,
because it is free.

XInputRumbleRideBand PUTS IT BACK. 1 restores the behaviour before this patch on
ridden packets, without a rebuild and without touching the thread's band, so if
frames ever regress on some title that is the one value to move -- and moving it
says which of these two halves was wrong rather than leaving both suspect.

NOT CLAIMED: that this makes the rumble stronger. It cannot. The peak is the
peak and the gain saturates at 255; what comes back is everything between the
peaks, which is where a rumble is actually felt.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.h
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.h
@@ -373,6 +373,21 @@
  * inert 0x31, sets the two bits the legacy motors need, and signs the packet
  * again. Returns len, or 0 for anything that is not a 78-byte 0x31. It does not
  * check inertness itself: the caller decides, and says why in its own line. */
+/* MGVF mgvf-0026: a percentage over one motor byte, saturating rather than
+ * wrapping, moved here from dualsense_usb.c because a second caller needs the
+ * same answer. The deadband has to know what a motor will be WHEN IT REACHES
+ * THE PAD, and the only way to know that is to do the arithmetic the rewrite
+ * will do. Two copies of three lines that must agree is how they stop
+ * agreeing. */
+static inline unsigned char dualsense_scale_motor(unsigned char motor, unsigned int gain)
+{
+    unsigned int scaled;
+
+    if (!motor) return motor;
+    scaled = ((unsigned int)motor * gain) / 100;
+    return scaled > 255 ? 255 : (unsigned char)scaled;
+}
+
 int dualsense_bt_output_is_inert(const unsigned char *report, unsigned int len);
 unsigned int dualsense_bt_stamp_motors(unsigned char *report, unsigned int len,
                                        unsigned char right, unsigned char left,
