+ *
+ * AND IT ASKED IT OF PACKETS THAT COST NOTHING. mgvf-0018 put the band there to
+ * protect a Bluetooth link that drains about sixty-five reports a second, and
+ * it was right to: every packet was ours and every one of them was a slot. That
+ * was BEFORE mgvf-0020. A change that rides the game's own packet spends no slot
+ * at all, so there is nothing to protect and holding it back buys nothing --
+ * it only throws away the texture the band was never meant to touch.
+ *
+ * So the band now applies to the writer that spends a slot, the thread, and
+ * compares the movement AT THE PAD. The ride carries everything the client
+ * asks, at the client's own rate, because that is free. What both still share
+ * is the line below it: a pair the pad already has is never worth a packet,
+ * whichever writer would send it.
+ *
+ * AND THE BAND IS WHY THIS DRIVER STOPPED COSTING FRAMES, so taking it off
+ * anything needs more than an argument. Here is the argument, and then the way
+ * back.
+ *
+ * mgvf-0018 took Beast of Reincarnation from 3-5 fps lost with visible stutter
+ * to 2 fps and none. It did that by not spending a slot on a link that drains
+ * about sixty-five reports a second: every change it held was a packet of OURS
+ * that did not go out.
+ *
+ * A RIDDEN PACKET IS NOT ONE OF OURS. Read dualsense_bt_set_output_report --
+ * the client's 0x31 is forwarded either way. Stamped, it goes from our copy;
+ * untouched, it goes from the client's own buffer. Same packet, same link, same
+ * slot, whichever branch runs. The band on the ride cannot change how much is
+ * offered to the radio. It can only decide whether the motors inside a packet
+ * that is leaving anyway are current or stale.
+ *
+ * What it does add is a CRC over 74 bytes on the packets it no longer skips,
